cartridge "ALICE IN ADVENTURELAND/BOB SPIRKO/1985"

' ALICE IN ADVENTURELAND
' A ColecoVision game based on "Alice" (title screen "ALICE ... BY
' SPIRKO") by Bob Spirko, Ahoy! January 1985 (Commodore 64 BASIC).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: Alice is lost in a maze of 6 levels of 6 x 6 rooms and must
' find the White Rabbit. Rooms are walled with hedges; doors are open,
' green gates or white gates (the green and the white key open them).
' Six keys lie about: the elevator key (the elevator in every north-west
' corner room goes to any level), the green key, the looking-glass key
' (looking glasses no longer throw you somewhere at random), the pits key
' (pits no longer drop you a level), the gryphon key (the gryphon can no
' longer steal a key) and the white key. Stairs lead up and down. Gold
' coins: every tenth coin buys the next key you lack, or, with all six,
' tells where the White Rabbit is. The Duchess's kitchen, the Hatter's
' teahouse and the Carpenter's diner each take a coin when you walk in.
' Falling into a pit may cost a coin. The cat on each level tells where
' one key is. The gryphon steals a key and hides it elsewhere.
'
' From the original (orig/alice.bas): the 216 rooms with their doors and
' things (DATA 9610-9665, through maze.py), the start room (X 4, Y 5,
' level 1), the six keys and what each does, the green and white gates,
' the elevator (needs its key, any level), the stairs, the pits (a level
' down, 70% chance of losing a coin, the landing room's thing happens),
' the looking glass (a random room on levels 1-5, X and Y 0-4), the
' gryphon's steal (a random key 7-12 with 11 counted as 12; nothing if
' the gryphon key is carried or the chosen key is not; the key is hidden
' in the next of the 15 places of DATA 9570) and the cat's hint (the key
' of the cat's level, by quarter NW/NE/SW/SE and level), the three shops
' (a coin each time, or "SORRY, LITTLE GIRL, BUT YOU NEED GOLD"), ten
' coins buy the lowest missing key or reveal the rabbit's quarter and
' level, three speeds, the room view with the map, level and gold beside
' it, and the messages.
'
' Liberties: the looking glass throws Alice only into rooms she can walk
' back from without keys (on the C64 it could drop her behind a locked
' gate for good, e.g. level 1, X 4, Y 0). Alice walks freely in the room with the stick instead of
' pressing keys for single steps (the speeds are 1, 1.5 and 2 pixels a
' frame); she walks 24 pixels in before a room's thing happens, as on the
' C64. The map beside the room shows the rooms of the level you have
' visited and where you are (the C64 showed only the quarter). The gryphon
' swoops across the room at Alice's height after a warning and she can
' step out of its way (on the C64 it nearly always caught her). With the
' pits key Alice is asked whether to jump into a pit (the only way into
' the White Rabbit's room is a pit, so the pits key made the game
' unwinnable). The stairs and the elevator do not ask again in the room
' you arrive in. A locked gate says it is locked. No save/continue (no
' tape or disk); no quit key. The McDuchess burger-chain sign became
' "DUCHESS'S KITCHEN" (no restaurant chain's slogan); the decorations are
' tiles. The end shows the rooms walked through.
'
' Controls: either controller; the stick walks; a button says yes, the
' stick says no; in the elevator up/down choose the level and a button
' goes. On the title, up and down choose, a button starts or changes the
' speed.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Nothing flashes.

